"""Recognize short fa/far spellings and syllables split across token boundaries.

'Far' has the largest response. Degree/question words immediately before it
usually suppress it. A split f|ar is stronger than most other f|a syllables;
nasal continuations (f|an, f|n) tend to have the opposite sign. Longer words
such as 'family' are not treated as the short fragment 'fa'.
Smaller rules cover 'much', 'free', and fa-word families. Capitalization,
word boundaries, and preceding digits distinguish fragments and initialisms.
Transliteration extends the split-syllable rules to other alphabets.
